# AI Guardrail System — Structure & How to Change It

This rule documents how the repo tells AI agents how to work, so that any future change to the guardrail system stays consistent. Read it before you touch a rule, an entry file, a skill, a rule template, or the MCP config.

## Provenance layout (ours vs third-party, universal vs project)

Everything **we** author lives under an `anwaltde/` folder, split by reach:

- `anwaltde/universal/` — not repo-specific; liftable to **any** repo (e.g. language policy, markdown, this meta-rule). "Universal" means universal, not "universal within one team or organisation".
- `anwaltde/project/` — only valid in this repo (stack- or codebase-specific).

**Third-party / vendor** skills (`impeccable`, `nuxt-ui`, `ponytail`) stay **outside** `anwaltde/`. This is organizational only — Cursor auto-loads `.cursor/rules/**` regardless of subfolder; attach is still decided by `globs` / `alwaysApply`. The point: in every repo you can see at a glance what is ours and what is universally reusable.

There are exactly these **two reach classes**. Resist adding a third (e.g. a "stack" class) on spec: a rule is project-specific until it can be shown, individually, to hold in any repo. Classification comes out of going through a rule, not out of a blanket judgement.

## Where everything lives

| Artifact | Path | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Canonical rules | `.cursor/rules/anwaltde/{universal,project}/*.mdc` | Single source of truth per guardrail. Cursor auto-loads them via `globs` / `alwaysApply` (folder is organizational). |
| Universal entry file | `AGENTS.md` (repo root) | Tool-neutral index **and** the repo's only configuration layer. Fixed slot structure (see below). Holds values and pointers, never restated rule content. |
| Claude Code shim | `CLAUDE.md` (repo root) | Thin shim: `@AGENTS.md` + one `@import` per always-on rule (full `anwaltde/…` path). No content of its own. |
| Our skills | `.agents/skills/anwaltde/{universal,project}/**/SKILL.md` | Reusable workflows we author. Each needs a `name` and `description`. |
| Third-party skills | `.agents/skills/<vendor>/**/SKILL.md` (outside `anwaltde/`) | Vendor/community skills (`impeccable`, `nuxt-ui`, `ponytail`). Left as-is. |
| MCP config | `.mcp.json` (versioned) · `.cursor/mcp.json` (local, gitignored) | Only `.mcp.json` travels with the repo — see below. Secrets via `${ENV}`, never inline. Which servers belong here is itself a configuration question. |
| Decision memory | `.aura/ai-setup/{index,decisions,adaptations}.md` (committed, not gitignored) | Shared record for `ai-setup`/`ai-sync`: which decision was made and why, which `project/` version was adapted from what. Sits next to, not inside, the `.aura/docs/` bundle so it survives every doc run. Not ingested into Aura — its readers are the two skills. Absent → both skills treat the repo as never set up and start from a clean decision history; this never blocks a run. |

## MCP servers — one versioned file, one local one

**`.mcp.json` is the repo's statement of which servers this project needs.** It is committed, so a fresh clone inherits the set. `.cursor/mcp.json` is **gitignored** and belongs to the individual machine: a developer may reach the same servers through a user-level Cursor configuration instead, and that is fine. So the requirement is *not* "keep two files identical" — an uncommitted file cannot carry a repo invariant. It is: **every server a rule or skill calls is declared in `.mcp.json`, and the local setup actually provides it.** A server missing there is a call no clone can make.

The listing under the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → `## Tooling` names the servers and *why* each one is needed. Two reasons are distinct and both legitimate:

- **The tracker's own server**, without which the adapter `tracker-selection` pulls describes calls that cannot happen. This one is load-bearing.
- **A server needed for something other than the lifecycle** — reading a legacy tracker, a docs source, the PR host. A repo on one tracker may perfectly well keep another tracker's server for read access, and that is a deliberate choice, not leftover configuration. Say so in the listing, so the next reader does not "clean it up".

## Rule templates — carried, not run

A repo sometimes has to keep a rule it does not use: one that belongs to a variant of the workflow another repo runs (e.g. a Jira adapter in an Aura-only repo). Such a rule stays in the rule tree, `.cursor/rules/anwaltde/{universal,project}/*.mdc`, like any other — there is no separate template folder any more (AURA-1647 retired the previous carrier-only folder once conditional rules got a real central home in the blueprint itself).

**The rule tree itself carries the inertness**, structurally: `alwaysApply: false` and no `globs` mean Cursor never auto-attaches the file, and it is not agent-requestable through the rule map because no work-area row points at it. A template's own header states, in one line, that it is a template and under which condition it becomes active (e.g. "meaningful only when the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → `Tracker` names `jira`").

**The canonical origin of a conditional rule is the central blueprint**, `blueprint/rules/**` in the `engineering-foundation` wiki (see AURA-1062/1647): its manifest entry there carries a `condition: { key: <AGENTS.md key>, equals: <value> }` naming exactly when it applies. A repo whose configuration matches the condition installs the file into its own rule tree with a real attach mode (`globs` or `alwaysApply: true`) — that is the act that turns a carried template into a running rule; a repo whose configuration does not match keeps it inert, or does not carry it at all.

Consequences, all intended:

- A template is **not** listed in the rule map or the always-on list, and has no `@import`, as long as it stays inert.
- Adopting a template is an act: flip its attach mode, fill in the matching `## Configuration` keys, and add the MCP servers it needs — never a side effect of the file merely existing in the tree.

## AGENTS.md — the entry file and the configuration layer

`AGENTS.md` is read natively by Cursor and, via the `CLAUDE.md` shim, by Claude Code. Other agent tools increasingly read it too — check per tool rather than assuming it.

It carries two things and nothing else: an **index** of what to read where, and the repo's **configuration values**. Do **not** restate rule bodies here, and do **not** put `@import` lines here — `@import` is Claude-Code-only syntax and is dead text in every other tool.

### Fixed slots, then one free section

These headings appear in this order in **every** repo, even when a slot is empty:

| Slot | Answers | Content |
|---|---|---|
| Title + intro | What is this file? | One paragraph: index **and** configuration layer, not a rulebook |
| `## Configuration` | Which values apply here? | The value tables (see below), grouped |
| `## Required reading` | What must be read before planning work? | **Strictly neutral in the template** — it asks the question and names no file; every repo has an answer, even if it is "nothing" |
| `## Always-on rules` | What applies everywhere? | Names + one sentence of purpose each, no rule content |
| `## Rule map` | What do I read before which work? | Area → rule, with `Origin` |
| `## Skills` | Which workflows exist? | Inventory |
| `## Tooling` | What do I work with? | MCP servers, command overview |
| `## Project specifics` | *(deliberately nothing)* | **Outside the template** — free content, may be empty |

The last slot is what keeps the template a template. Without an explicit place for repo-specific content, it settles between the fixed slots and is indistinguishable from the template on the next transfer. Everything above `## Project specifics` is template; everything below belongs to this repo alone.

Do not put a tool name into a fixed slot. A command overview under `## Tooling` is universal; "Taskfile cheatsheet" is one repo's answer to it.

### Values are tables, not prose

Each row: **key · value · what it answers · owning rule**. Human-maintainable, agent-readable, checkable line by line. No YAML/frontmatter block — `AGENTS.md` is read by several tools that treat frontmatter differently.

The "owning rule" column makes *one canonical owner* visible in the configuration layer itself, and it splits the two halves of a key cleanly:

- the **value** lives here, in `AGENTS.md`;
- the **behaviour** — what the value means, and above all what happens when it is **missing** — lives in the owning rule.

A configuration value is therefore **not** a restated rule body, and the ban above does not apply to it. Prose that explains *how* something works has landed in the wrong file; a value plus one sentence of what it answers has not.

Two hard requirements for a row:

- The owning rule must **exist**. A row pointing at a rule that is planned but not written is a dead reference.
- Absence must be defined and **must not block**. A missing value degrades the work — it never stops it. "Degrades" also has to leave something behind: if the fallback removes the whole point of a step, the default is not degenerate, it is empty, and the key or the step is wrong.

## CLAUDE.md — the Claude Code shim

Claude Code does not read `AGENTS.md` natively, so `CLAUDE.md` wires it in:

```markdown
# CLAUDE.md
@AGENTS.md
@.cursor/rules/anwaltde/universal/general-language-policy.mdc
@.cursor/rules/anwaltde/universal/general-english-comments.mdc
@.cursor/rules/anwaltde/universal/general-db-destructive-ops.mdc
```

Rules:

- One `@import` line per **`alwaysApply: true`** rule — never for glob-scoped rules (that would bloat every session). Glob-scoped rules are surfaced via the rule-map.
- Use the **full path** including the `anwaltde/{universal,project}/` folder.
- `@import` lines must **not** sit inside a fenced code block, or Claude Code will not evaluate them.

## Rules — location, naming, frontmatter

- Location: `.cursor/rules/anwaltde/{universal,project}/*.mdc`. One guardrail per file, no duplication. Pick `universal/` if the rule holds in any repo, `project/` if it is stack- or codebase-specific.
- Naming: a domain prefix in kebab-case, English filename — `general-`, `backend-`, `frontend-`, `tracker-`, … The list is **open**: the prefix names the domain the rule guards. Do not treat it as a closed enumeration, and do not name a prefix after a product where the domain will outlive it.
- Frontmatter: always a `description`, plus exactly one **attach mode**:
  - `globs` (file-scoped auto-attach), **or**
  - `alwaysApply: true` (applies everywhere), **or**
  - neither — an **agent-requestable** rule that is pulled in on demand and surfaced via the rule-map (e.g. `frontend-index`).
  Never set both `globs` **and** `alwaysApply: true`.
- Body shape: short *Why* → ✅/❌ examples → a verification checklist.
- Language: English (see `general-language-policy`).

### A rule carries guidance, not history

A rule says what to do and why it holds — not how it came to be. Ticket numbers, incident dates, and "this was decided because back then …" belong in the decision log, not in a guardrail that is injected into every session that matches its globs.

The one exception: a **concrete incident** whose retelling is what makes the rule stick (a data loss, an outage). Then keep it short and keep it in service of the instruction.

Where the history goes is a configuration question — see the `Decision-log location` key in the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → `## Configuration`. When you move archaeology out of a rule, put it there; do not delete it.

## Skills

Skills we author live in `.agents/skills/anwaltde/{universal,project}/**/SKILL.md` (same `universal`/`project` split as rules). Third-party/vendor skills stay outside `anwaltde/`. Each skill needs a `name` and a `description` in its frontmatter; the slash-command derives from `name`, not from the folder path, so moving a skill does not change its command. A skill carries a *workflow*; a rule carries a *guardrail*. Keep that split: do not turn a skill into a rule or vice versa.

## MCP servers are configuration, not a fixed list

Which MCP servers a repo pins depends on what it actually works with — a repo that does not use a tracker has no business pinning that tracker's server. Treat the two MCP files as an answer to a configuration question, not as a set to be copied: when a rule template is adopted, its servers come with it; when a workflow is dropped, its servers go.

## How to add a NEW rule (do this in order)

1. Decide reach: `universal/` (any repo) or `project/` (only here). This picks the folder under `.cursor/rules/anwaltde/`. If the rule is one this repo carries but does not run, it is a **template**: same folder, `alwaysApply: false`, no `globs`, and a header line naming its activating condition — skip steps 5 and 6 while it stays inert.
2. Pick the domain prefix and a kebab-case English name: `<domain>-<topic>.mdc`.
3. Write the frontmatter: a `description`, plus exactly one attach mode — `globs` (the files it guards), `alwaysApply: true` (applies everywhere), or neither (agent-requestable, pulled in on demand). Never both `globs` and `alwaysApply: true`.
4. Write the body: *Why* → ✅/❌ examples → checklist. English only, guidance not history.
5. Add a row to the **rule-map in `AGENTS.md`** (area → this rule) including the `Origin` (`u`/`p`). The map paths must match the `globs` in the frontmatter exactly.
6. If — and only if — the rule is `alwaysApply: true`, add it to the **always-on list in `AGENTS.md`** *and* add one `@import` line (full path) to `CLAUDE.md`.
7. If the rule reads a project-specific value, add that value as a row in the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → `## Configuration` with this rule as its owner, and define in the rule what happens when it is missing.
8. If the rule supersedes or overlaps an existing one, resolve the overlap (one canonical owner; the other references it) so no guardrail lives in two files.

## Adaptation convention — moving a rule or skill from `universal/` to `project/`

A `universal/` rule or skill is liftable, but not always usable unchanged. Adapting one for this repo is a **move**, never a copy: the universal original is deleted from this repo as part of the same change. Leaving both versions in place would, for a glob-scoped rule, make Cursor attach both and hand the agent two conflicting instructions on the same topic — and it removes the one thing a central comparison needs, a single unambiguous local version to compare against.

A move is an **atomic** operation, not a file relocation with cleanup to follow. Three mechanical follow-ups belong in the **same** change, because each breaks something silently if it is missing:

| Follow-up | What breaks if it is missing |
|---|---|
| `@import` line in `CLAUDE.md` (for an always-on rule) | The import points at nothing; the always-on guarantee silently stops applying for Claude Code |
| `Origin` column of the rule-map in `AGENTS.md` (`u` → `p`) | The map claims liftability that no longer exists |
| Path in the skill inventory (the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → `## Skills`) | The slash command points at a folder that is gone |

The decision-memory file `adaptations.md` (see the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → `## Configuration`, the decision-memory-path key this rule owns) records, per adapted file: its **origin** (which universal original it came from), the **source version** at the time of adaptation, the **reason**, and the **scope of deviation**. This is the only place the adaptation is marked — not the file's own frontmatter, because the `project/` folder already states *that* something is project-specific; only the log states *what it came from* and *why*, which a comparison against the central set needs to tell a deliberate adaptation from a stale copy.

## How to rename or remove a rule

- Rename/move: update the filename and/or `universal`↔`project` folder, every cross-reference (grep the old name and old path repo-wide), the rule-map row (incl. `Origin`), any `## Configuration` row naming it as owner, and — if always-on — the `@import` line in `CLAUDE.md`.
- Remove: delete the file, its rule-map row, any `@import`, any `## Configuration` row owned by it, and all references.

Rename and reference catch-up belong in the **same** change. In between, every reference points at nothing — and because references use full paths, nothing finds the rule. A branch in that state is not a valid stopping point.

## Verification checklist before changing guardrails

- [ ] Exactly one canonical file owns the guardrail (no duplication).
- [ ] The rule sits under `anwaltde/universal/` or `anwaltde/project/` by its reach; if the repo carries but does not run it, it stays in the same tree, inert via `alwaysApply: false` and no `globs`, with a header naming its activating condition.
- [ ] Frontmatter has a `description` and never both `globs` and `alwaysApply: true` (glob-scoped, always-on, or agent-requestable).
- [ ] The rule-map in `AGENTS.md` is updated (incl. `Origin`) and its paths match the `globs`.
- [ ] `alwaysApply` rules have a full-path `@import` in `CLAUDE.md`; glob rules do not.
- [ ] Every project-specific value the rule reads is a `## Configuration` row that names this rule as owner, and the rule defines the non-blocking behaviour when it is absent.
- [ ] `AGENTS.md` still has the fixed slots in the fixed order, and nothing repo-specific sits above `## Project specifics`.
- [ ] A `universal/` → `project/` adaptation is a move (no leftover universal version), landed together with its three follow-ups (`@import`, rule-map `Origin`, skill-inventory path), and logged in `adaptations.md`.
- [ ] The body carries guidance, not archaeology; anything historical went to the decision log.
- [ ] All content is English; no old rule name or path remains anywhere.
